    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_13b48a67e430bc980eb507cf206e3aae87f0e0e125d89e6c0bf1e96f2e2d8480",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The span uses mollification as a black-box family method: after splitting level-varying newforms by functional-equation sign, it yields positive-proportion nonvanishing of central values in one subfamily and central derivatives in the other, together with an average vanishing-order bound. Those analytic events acquire arithmetic or spectral meaning only through additional bridge results, producing winding-quotient and rank consequences and informing Maass-form deformation. Family aggregation gains detectable nonvanishing for a proportion of forms, but neither proves full nonvanishing nor exposes the mollifier's internal mechanism in this evidence boundary.",
