    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_36349cd472da9d1ef9de5cc6f1174b2db3ab5549572b91de817977f73d0dd23c",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Burgess conductor-aspect subconvexity is routed through short Dirichlet-character sums: shifts are aggregated, large even powers are taken, and the resulting expressions permit use of complete character-sum bounds resting on Weil's proved function-field RH for curves. The representation change trades a short incomplete sum for more complicated complete sums, gaining access to stronger geometric cancellation estimates. Its reach depends on Dirichlet-character structure and imported function-field bounds; it does not prove GRH for the original Dirichlet L-function, even though it is nontrivial in some ranges where that GRH yields no nontrivial short-sum estimate.",
