    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_4728684e221d1c7ce26911e3d3e1d0bc9be7695e9488e7b9839332160811582f",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The source contrasts two scaling regimes. For a fixed cuspidal form on GL_m over Q—restricted to Q there so that its L-function does not factor further—and assuming GRH, unfolding high zeros to mean spacing one removes changing density and reveals CUE-type universal local statistics in restricted proven ranges. Low-lying zeros sampled across a family instead retain family symmetry, with different monodromy types producing different laws tied to central nonvanishing fractions and moment predictions. In function fields Frobenius and monodromy make this bridge structural; over number fields the comparison rests on restricted analytic results and computation, not on an identified monodromy group.",
